00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:11.670
What are you invested in?

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:14.550
A lot of these banks hadn't
invested in risky assets.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.050
It wasn't like First Republic

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:18.390
or even Silicon Valley Bank was invested

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:21.300
in a lot of, you know,
credit derivative, swaps

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:22.710
and things like that,

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:24.900
weren't invested in crypto
and things like that.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:26.730
These were a lot of treasuries,

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:28.080
mortgage-backed securities.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.420
What happened was they
had taken huge losses

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:32.610
because the Federal
Reserve had raised rates by

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:35.370
at really the fastest pace
in history when you judge

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:38.419
by how low rates were and
how high they are now.

00:06:38.419 --> 00:06:40.440
This is the fastest rate hiking cycle

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:41.790
in history for the Fed.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:44.160
And that cuts into the
value of those bonds.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.920
And so, safe things like
mortgage-backed securities

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:50.217
and treasuries become unsafe
because you lose value.

00:06:50.217 --> 00:06:53.264
And a lot of these banks
weren't writing that value down.

00:06:53.264 --> 00:06:55.320
And so, as they had to take

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.600
on more cash to hold up and pay for that

00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:59.850
and give money back to their
customers, because again,

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:01.890
all those deposits are owned by the bank.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.470
They're owned by their
customers, their liabilities,

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:06.195
meaning that the bank
has to give them back

00:07:06.195 --> 00:07:08.160
if customers want them.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:09.240
And customers were coming saying,

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:10.890
hey, give me my money back.

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:12.270
That means the bank had to come up

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.370
with the money for those
losses they'd suffered

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:16.080
in the market from the treasuries

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.240
and mortgage back
securities, things like that.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.971
But now we're shifting into
a new phase where I think

00:07:20.971 --> 00:07:22.800
there's gonna be more worry about,

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:23.910
hey, what do you own?

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:25.500
Are you invested in any risky stuff?

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:26.970
Are you hedging?

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:28.680
Are you properly covering your risk?

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:30.094
Because that was what wasn't happening

00:07:30.094 --> 00:07:31.830
at Silicon Valley Bank.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:34.783
And I think the fact that First
Republic did not have a plan

00:07:34.783 --> 00:07:37.650
in place before they
reported an earnings loss

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:40.170
of a hundred billion in deposits,

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:41.910
well, that suggests that
maybe they weren't doing

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:43.800
their due diligence as bankers as well.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:46.080
And that I think is gonna be
the question for investors.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:47.670
Are you doing your due diligence?

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:49.020
Are you insuring your deposits?

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:51.180
Are you doing and taking
the steps necessary

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:53.430
so you don't suffer a bunch of big losses?

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:55.140
That I think segues us perfectly

