00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:46.680
So, I wanna put something up here

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:48.240
on your screen next, right here.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:49.440
And just so we can talk

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:51.690
about what's happening in the markets.

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:53.730
Now, as we look out at the markets,

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:55.200
this is the US treasury yield curve

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:58.470
and that shows us how much
bonds are yielding right now.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.960
As you see on the left
side of your screen there,

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:03.390
that's one month to six month to one year.

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:05.850
Now a treasury yield curve is supposed

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:08.310
to look basically the opposite of this.

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:10.440
It's kind of, it's generally
either a straight line

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:11.850
or something like a Nike swoosh

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:13.950
where if you're loan
in the government money

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:16.050
because that's what
these treasury bonds are,

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:17.220
they're loans to the government.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:18.960
Well, if think about
loaning someone money,

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:21.660
if I tell you I'm gonna pay you
back this a hundred dollars,

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:24.330
you loaned me in a month, you'd
say, okay, well, you know,

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:25.410
maybe gimme a dollar,

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:27.810
maybe gimme $2 just to
make up for my time.

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:30.570
But if I say, okay, well, I'm gonna get

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:31.410
this a hundred dollars from you,

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:32.850
I'm gonna pay you back in 30 years.

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:34.320
Or you're probably like,
whoa, whoa, whoa, 30 years.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:36.030
I don't know what's
gonna happen in 30 years.

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:37.380
You gotta make it worth my while.

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:39.240
You're gonna have to pay me 5, 10 bucks.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:40.530
You know, for that.

00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:41.550
That's usually what happens.

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:43.560
So, as the further you
get out on the curve,

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:45.384
the higher interest payments usually are.

00:09:45.384 --> 00:09:47.400
The opposite is happening right now

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:49.530
because there's fear in the markets

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:50.730
and there's the expectations

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:52.770
that the Federal Reserve is
going to keep raising rates.

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:56.040
And so, you see 1 month,
2 months, 3, 4, 6 months,

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:58.620
all around that 5% level going

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:00.720
from two months to about six months

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:03.330
and then one year you
